{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central claim that preferring a better-corroborated theory 'inevitably smuggles induction back' rests on a false dichotomy: it assumes the only possible justification for such a preference is an inductive belief that past success predicts future truth (probability). This ignores Popper's explicit non-inductive justification based on verisimilitude (truth-likeness) and critical rationality. Popper argues we prefer the better-corroborated theory not because we believe it is more likely to be true in the future, but because it has withstood the most severe attempts at falsification, making it the best candidate for closeness to the truth *now*. The target conflates 'rational preference for action' with 'inductive inference of probability,' thereby attacking a straw man of Popper's position rather than engaging with his actual argument that one can act on the best-tested theory without assuming the uniformity of nature.","counterexample":["A scientist chooses Theory A over Theory B because A has survived a test that B failed, not because they believe A will definitely hold tomorrow, but because B is known to be false while A remains the only viable candidate for truth."],"premise":"Popper distinguishes between corroboration (a report on past performance) and probability (a prediction of future frequency), arguing that preference is based on the former's indication of verisimilitude, not an inductive leap.","successor_question":"If the preference is not based on the expectation of future success derived from the past, how does Popper justify the practical utility of choosing the better-corroborated theory without implicitly relying on some form of inductive reliability?","target":"8b902069cd4a92545af148d740eca1bdcf7a116e7075a9e67f139514642b6337"}]}